WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.880
- Drugs is like what everybody turns to.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.310
And then, like I said, they
call this the "City of Hope."

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.350
I remember one time somebody
took some spray paint

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.710
and knocked off the H and put a D there.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.120
And made it to say the sign

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.170
that coming in saying the "City of Dope."

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.150
- [Interviewer] All right Matthew.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.030
Matthew, where are you from originally?

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:24.863
Where were you born?

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:27.580
- I was born in Manchester, Kentucky.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.527
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your family.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:30.335
You had both your parents growing up?

00:00:30.335 --> 00:00:33.190
- I did until I was about four,

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.840
and then I have a
stepdad who actually used

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.663
to be married to my dad's sister.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.890
So my mom left my dad,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.890
and my aunt and her husband got split up,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.680
which would be my uncle by marriage.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.680
And my mom and my, you know,
used to be uncle by marriage,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.700
or had been married for 20
some years now since '96.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.660
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your childhood.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.507
- So growing up in Clay County,
it was pretty, you know,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.800
it was pretty boring childhood.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.560
- [Interviewer] Tell me about Clay County.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.393
- Clay County.

00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:17.110
- [Interviewer] It's one
of the poorest counties

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:17.943
in the country.

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:18.776
- Absolutely.

00:01:18.776 --> 00:01:20.160
They call it the "City of Hope,"

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.321
but really it's pretty hopeless here.

00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:26.596
And you know, sometimes I
pray that for all the kids

00:01:26.596 --> 00:01:28.690
around here, because there's just,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.890
it is a really tough place to grow up.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.120
There was no positive
role models, you know,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.430
anywhere you go.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.324
There's very few positive role models.

00:01:37.324 --> 00:01:40.150
Let me put it that way, there's a couple.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.730
But you know, it's just not a place

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.083
for that I feel kids should be.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.510
- [Interviewer] Very few jobs.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:47.938
- No jobs at all.

00:01:47.938 --> 00:01:51.280
You know, the jobs that
are here are sold up.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.340
You do have to go at least 45 minutes

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.920
to the towns around here,
and you might get lucky

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.011
and find a job.

00:01:57.011 --> 00:01:59.470
But even those are rare.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.715
And you know, if you get a
job there and messing up,

00:02:02.715 --> 00:02:05.050
you don't have no options after that.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.410
So, usually everybody will mess

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.760
up their first couple of jobs.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.930
And so, yeah, it's pretty
rough on the work around here.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.409
- [Interviewer] So you
got how far in school?

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:17.930
- I was in the seventh grade.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.443
I failed the seventh grade four times.

00:02:20.443 --> 00:02:23.770
And they automatically
moved me to the ninth grade.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.827
And I did officially make
it to the 10th grade,

00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:31.120
when that should have been my senior year.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.070
'Cause they moved me up a few grades.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.050
But I got expelled every
year since the seventh grade.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.180
There wasn't one year that
I ever got to see the end

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.013
of a school year.

00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:42.281
I got the full expulsion.

00:02:42.281 --> 00:02:43.920
- [Interviewer] For what?

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.220
- Fighting, drugs.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.473
A little bit of everything.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.860
- [Interviewer] So you got
into drugs at what age?

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.070
- When I was a very young, you know.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:59.610
The people around me, drugs is
real normal around kids here.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.470
You know I remember school teachers

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.780
from down here coming to
buy drugs from my parents.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.780
And I would go to school and
be there and see the teachers,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.890
I know that they'd come to,
it was just real normal.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.406
By the time I was five like
you can hand me any pill

00:03:16.406 --> 00:03:19.410
that's out there and
make you say what's this,

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.920
and I could say that's a
Xanax, that's a Percocet,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:23.830
that's a Lortab.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.563
So I knew, you know, I
was raised around it.

00:03:26.563 --> 00:03:28.410
- [Interviewer] At five years old?

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.243
- Yeah.

00:03:29.243 --> 00:03:30.590
So, you know, it was.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.480
- [Interviewer] And pills
were big then, right?

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.150
- Absolutely, there was no heroin

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.843
or nothing down here that was.

00:03:36.843 --> 00:03:39.580
Oxycontin was the biggest thing.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.860
And then the Percocet 30's,

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.390
and they absolutely destroyed this place.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.570
And the churches tried to get together,

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.232
and places tried to
get together to make it

00:03:50.232 --> 00:03:53.660
to fight against the drugs and stuff.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.479
But even our government
like totally went downhill

00:03:57.479 --> 00:03:59.979
in this town.

00:03:59.979 --> 00:04:04.979
We had the mayor go to jail.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.750
All kinds of stuff, you
know like, they buy votes.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.720
You can get a vote for 20 bucks down here.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.500
And there's people that all
these elected officials,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.041
they hand out money.

00:04:17.041 --> 00:04:20.670
And so say you're a runner to get votes.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:21.940
They'll hand you a stack of money

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.930
and you go get everybody you can,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.480
hey, I got 20 bucks,
jump in the car with me

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.320
and I'll take you and you vote

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.510
for who I want you to vote for.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.620
So like, this whole town is twisted.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.610
Like even the people
that are in authority,

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:38.492
just ain't altogether neither.

00:04:38.492 --> 00:04:43.011
- [Interviewer] So where'd
your life go after that?

00:04:43.011 --> 00:04:48.011
- So after, you know,
when I was 18 years old,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:53.070
I went to jail the first
time, the big boy jail.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.750
Adult jail here in Clay County
for something really stupid.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.200
Got out in a couple hours.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.513
And then shortly after that,

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.940
I went to jail for a felonious assault.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.390
They said I broke somebody's legs.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.470
And it was really a self-defense,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.832
and I didn't touch the guy's legs.

00:05:12.832 --> 00:05:16.950
He just fell and couldn't get
back up after he attacked me,

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.240
and I like kicked him off on me.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.890
So my punishment for that was,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.756
it was a class B felony,

00:05:25.756 --> 00:05:29.563
and that's five to 10
years here in Kentucky.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.190
So my punishment, they made a deal

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.561
with me that they would
drop it to a misdemeanor,

00:05:35.561 --> 00:05:39.000
put me on unsupervised probation.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.950
The terms of the probation

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.120
were to stay out of Clay
County for three years.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.180
And they've actually, my
parents were at that time

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.020
we were already kicked out

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.673
for five years both of
them for trafficking.

00:05:49.673 --> 00:05:52.170
So like, you know, that's
something they do a lot,

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.120
and just make people leave Clay County

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.020
when they get in trouble.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.770
- [Interviewer] Rather
than putting in prison,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.320
they just ship them out?

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:00.660
- Yup, yes sir.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.660
But so then after that,
I go to Hamilton, Ohio,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.863
and I did five years in prison down there

00:06:08.863 --> 00:06:11.080
for robbing a bank.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.920
You know, it all stems from drugs.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:17.920
Like some of the people you
met today, when I was 12,

00:06:18.558 --> 00:06:21.910
like I had people, adults
stick a needle in my arm

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.937
and shoot me up with cocaine,
when I was 12 years old.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.560
I may have been 13.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.900
I'm not quite sure the age but, you know,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:30.860
people that were supposed

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.180
to be my role models stuck
needles in my arm when I was 13.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:37.750
So that's, you know.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.968
And I was in places where they cooked meth

00:06:40.968 --> 00:06:43.947
for the first time at about 14 down here.

00:06:43.947 --> 00:06:46.375
And that was huge down here, meth.

00:06:46.375 --> 00:06:48.170
'Cause it was a lot
cheaper than the pills,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:49.530
and it was a lot more affordable.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.940
'Cause like I said,
there's poverty around,

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.560
nothing but poverty.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.000
People are waiting at
their mailboxes every first

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.250
of the month waiting on a check.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.440
Like if you got it on
the first and the third,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.270
there's lines at the mailbox
yelling at each other,

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.880
hey, everybody just waiting for the check

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.280
because that's how everybody
survives down here.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.553
So yeah.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.740
- [Interviewer] What drugs are
most popular today down here?

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.423
- Most popular today is meth by far.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.763
I have heard of heroin
being down here and.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.820
But meth is like got this place destroyed.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.310
Everybody's shooting up
meth, and it's crazy.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.310
It went from OC 80's and
cocaine to meth and heroin.

00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:39.476
- [Interviewer] And how old are you now?

00:07:39.476 --> 00:07:40.338
- I am 29.

00:07:40.338 --> 00:07:41.171
- [Interviewer] 29.

00:07:41.171 --> 00:07:42.650
And you've done a little prison time?

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:43.483
- Yes sir.

00:07:43.483 --> 00:07:44.670
I did five years.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.400
- [Interviewer] Are you
working or have you worked?

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.690
- I own a business.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:48.960
When I was 24.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.030
You know my dad actually owned

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.060
a business here in Clay County.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.840
They called him Jack the Tire Man.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.833
He was also one of the elected
official I was referring to.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:04.350
He owned the tire shop and he
was elected as the constable.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.900
And shortly after he was elected

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.003
for the constable, he actually.

00:08:10.065 --> 00:08:12.097
I went to prison and I talked to him,

00:08:12.097 --> 00:08:15.220
and then three months
after I was in jail waiting

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.920
to go to prison, I got a
phone call that the feds

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.960
had got my dad, and he
was an elected official,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.981
and he got 20 years
for selling one Lortab,

00:08:23.981 --> 00:08:26.060
and he had a gun.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:27.340
And they charged him career criminal

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.083
and they gave him 20 years
over one pill down here.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:32.420
- [Interviewer] So you robbed a bank?

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.040
- Yes, I robbed a bank.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:36.900
- [Interviewer] And how
many banks have you robbed?

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:38.173
- I got caught for one.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:40.493
- [Interviewer] Good answer.

00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:41.610
- It hasn't.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:45.600
You know, like I did
make a deal that a lot

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.600
of stuff that I'd done,
in part of the deal I made

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.890
for five years, anything
that got brought up

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:58.040
after I did my time wouldn't be, you know,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.410
they couldn't hold against me.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.110
But the thing is, that's
only in the state of Ohio.

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:04.757
So, you know.

00:09:04.757 --> 00:09:06.280
- [Interviewer] What
goes through your head

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.303
as you're planning to rob a bank?

00:09:08.303 --> 00:09:09.750
What's the situation?

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.600
You're desperate for something, drugs?

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.631
- No, it was more of an adrenaline thing.

00:09:14.631 --> 00:09:19.540
So I had been planning on
robbing a bank for six months.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.927
I'd been looking at this
bank for six months.

00:09:21.927 --> 00:09:24.280
And I actually knew a
couple of bank robbers

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.740
that got me into it.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:31.617
You know, getaway driver, a guy
who's robbed multiple banks.

00:09:31.617 --> 00:09:34.895
And you know, the getaway
driver and another guy my age

00:09:34.895 --> 00:09:37.383
that was their first bank
they'd robbed with him.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:42.904
And so I've known some bank
robbers and I watched them,

00:09:42.904 --> 00:09:45.650
you know, and they
talked to me and told me

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.067
about the adrenaline you get
from it and how you can go in

00:09:48.067 --> 00:09:51.930
and in 30 seconds come out
with all kinds of money.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.756
So, and you know, it was a good story.

00:09:53.756 --> 00:09:56.470
They also was talking
about how they took care

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.970
of their family after
they got out of there.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.097
It wasn't really, they made it
so glorious to me, you know,

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:03.380
and I was a young kid.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.720
And that's how I got into it.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:10.161
So when I was planning
to rob this certain bank

00:10:10.161 --> 00:10:14.370
for six months and had a great plan,

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.470
would've definitely, most
likely I would've got away

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:19.030
with it, can't say definitely.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.990
But I had a great plan.

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:23.970
But then one night I was
partying with some girls,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.700
and I had a bunch of money,

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:28.880
and they asked me how did
I keep money like that?

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.883
And I said, come with
me and I'll show you.

00:10:31.883 --> 00:10:35.310
And you know, I said take me to this bank.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:36.940
And they said, you know, what for?

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.720
And I said, I'm going
to withdraw some money.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.050
And that was the first time I got caught,

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:44.680
but I'd actually did 20 Xanax

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:49.040
and drunk a half a
fifth of Captain Morgan.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.340
And my plan went out the window.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.530
I went in with no masks, no nothing.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.996
Like I was so high on Xanax and drinking,

00:10:57.996 --> 00:11:01.889
that everything I was
taught went out the window.

00:11:01.889 --> 00:11:03.700
Everything I was taught

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:06.960
on how to not get caught robbing
a bank went out the window.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.930
No face, no case, no double doors.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.500
Watch the time limit.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.863
You know, everything went out the window.

00:11:14.863 --> 00:11:18.450
It was like I was watching out of my body

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:22.370
and watching from above like
on a video camera myself,

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:23.820
my body just reacted.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.690
And I went in, leaned over the counter,

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.000
this is a robbery, don't make it a murder.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.339
Give me all the hundreds and fifties.

00:11:32.339 --> 00:11:34.138
- [Interviewer] How
much did they give you?

00:11:34.138 --> 00:11:35.133
- Not enough.

00:11:35.133 --> 00:11:36.880
It's kind of embarrassing to say.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:38.803
They gave me five years to start with.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:40.970
And $2,800.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:45.970
So it was, like I said, I
wasn't in no shape to do it.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:51.260
I did get away for a couple more weeks,

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.660
but like I said I had some girls with me,

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.630
and that was the dumbest
thing I ever could have done.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.670
One of them I barely knew.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.870
So I got told on.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.830
I really, like I said, they knew my face,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.480
but nobody where we was at in the place

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:08.830
we was at knew who I was.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.411
So even if I had been all over the news,

00:12:11.411 --> 00:12:13.460
and everybody see me,

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.030
there's a really good chance
I could have got away with it.

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:19.310
But I had some people
that were local to me,

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:23.180
that were local to the
community that was with me.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.820
And one of them got scared
and they actually went

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.130
to the police and let them know who I was.

